# S0: measure before changing anything

The headless bench and the 1.20 baseline

Unit 1, talk 2

---

## The rule of S0

- Change nothing about how Hadur plays
- Build the ruler, then measure the robot you have
- Everything later is compared with this number

---

## How the bench works

- Runs the full Robocode 1.9.5.6 engine from Maven, headless
- One JVM per battle, a fixed random seed, security manager on
- A cold run: **35 rounds x 5 seeds** per opponent, learned data wiped
- The same command gives the same result, while no turn is skipped

---

## Ground truth on every turn

- The bench logs both robots' true position, energy and live bullets
- Later stages can be scored against what really happened
- Not what the robot thought happened

---

## The baseline table

| Opponent | Score share | Rounds won |
|---|---|---|
| sample.SpinBot | 99.2% | 174 / 175 |
| sample.Tracker | 99.5% | 175 / 175 |
| sample.Crazy | 99.8% | 175 / 175 |
| sample.Walls | 99.6% | 174 / 175 |
| sample.RamFire | 97.2% | 174 / 175 |
| **abc.Shadow 3.83c** | **40.8% ± 8.3** | **82 / 175** |

*Mean ± 95% interval over 5 seeds*

---

## The key finding

- Hadur wins **47%** of rounds against Shadow
- Yet it takes only **40.8%** of the score
- Shadow wins on damage (**36.5%** bullet-damage share for Hadur), even in rounds it loses

So score share became the headline metric. Shadow is the reference opponent. The sample bots are sanity checks.

---

## A problem the bench found early

- 1.20 skipped **44 turns** across 30 battles
- **31** of them against Shadow, about one every six rounds
- Some turns spiked to 43 ms

A skipped turn changes the robot's behaviour, so repeat runs with skips drift slightly. This became S6's tick-budget work.

---

## What it taught us

- Pick the metric that matches the goal before you tune anything
- A fixed seed turns a battle into an experiment you can repeat
- The bench finds problems nobody asked about (skipped turns)
- Honest intervals matter: 40.8% ± 8.3 is a wide band, so small changes will not show

